<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Empire of Nicaea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1204">1204</a>–<a href="/years/1261">1261</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Nicene Empire</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> was the largest of the three Greek rump states founded by the aristocracy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> after crusaders occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> in 1204. Established by the Laskaris family in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-i-laskaris">Theodore I Laskaris</a>, it assumed the traditional titles of the Byzantine emperors in 1205. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iii-doukas-vatatzes">John III Doukas Vatatzes</a> recovered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and Macedonia and incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. In July 1261 the general Alexios Strategopoulos entered Constantinople, restoring the empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>. By c. 1300 nearly the whole of the former Nicene lands had been conquered by the Turks.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This brought the brothers into conflict with Theodore I Laskaris, who was building the rival <b>Empire of Nicaea</b> in western Anatolia, and David even sought help from the Latin emperor in Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The founding of the Latin Empire created five polities at once that claimed to be the Roman Empire: the Latin Empire, the Holy Roman Empire, the Despotate of Epirus, the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b> and the Empire of Trebizond.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The following year, allied with Michael II of Epirus and Manfred of Sicily against the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b>, William was defeated and captured at the Battle of Pelagonia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaloyan's union with Rome lapsed under Ivan Asen II, who allied with the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b>, and in 1235 a council at Lampsacus recognised the archbishop of Tarnovo as patriarch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Kaykhusraw I was killed in 1211 fighting the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b> at Antioch on the Meander, but his sons expanded to the coasts: Kaykaus I took Sinop and captured the emperor of Trebizond, and Kayqubad I fortified Alanya, building its Red Tower and a shipyard, sent an expedition…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>George Mouzalon, a high official of the <b>Empire of Nicaea</b> under Theodore II Laskaris and its regent, was assassinated in a conspiracy of nobles led by Michael VIII Palaiologos, who became co-emperor in 1259 and recovered Constantinople in 1261.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
